<section class="subhero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">SimpleTen / Sacramento, California</div><h1 data-reveal="words">AI agents for Sacramento teams. Useful help, clear limits.</h1><p class="lede">Give customers and teammates a helpful starting point. We scope AI assistants around a specific job, approved information, and an easy path to a person.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="#contact">Discuss your workflow <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="/#blueprint">How the blueprint works</a></div></div></section>
<section class="section light"><div class="wrap article-grid"><div class="article-copy"><h2 data-reveal="focus">An assistant with a job it can do well.</h2><p>A useful AI agent needs a defined purpose. SimpleTen helps Sacramento service businesses plan assistants for intake, common questions, internal knowledge, and supported scheduling tasks. We decide what the assistant can access, what it can do, and when it should stop and ask a person.</p><h3 id="intake">Customer questions and intake</h3><p>Use approved business information to answer common questions, collect the details needed for an inquiry, and direct it to the right person. An assistant should be clear about its role and avoid inventing prices, availability, policies, or commitments.</p><h3 id="knowledge">Knowledge your team can find</h3><p>Knowledge OS starts with the documents and processes your team already trusts. We help organize that information and build an assistant that can retrieve relevant sources within agreed access limits. Questions without a reliable answer should be escalated rather than answered confidently.</p><h3 id="oversight">Human handoffs are part of the design</h3><p>We identify actions that need approval, test representative questions, and agree on how someone can review the interaction. Access to customer or business information is scoped to the task. The assistant supports your people, who remain responsible for important decisions.</p></div><aside class="scope-box"><h2>What we scope together</h2><ul><li>A clearly defined assistant role</li><li>Approved knowledge and source review</li><li>Permissions and action limits</li><li>Representative question testing</li><li>Human escalation and review</li><li>Documentation and maintenance options</li></ul><a class="button" href="#contact">Start with an assessment <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><small>Deliverables, timing, software costs, and ongoing support are agreed before a paid build begins.</small></aside></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap example-grid"><div class="example-copy"><span class="example-label">Example use case / illustrative</span><h2 data-reveal="words">A new teammate needs an answer.</h2><p>An internal assistant can help a new employee find an approved process without interrupting a colleague each time. The goal is a traceable answer within their access permissions.</p></div><ol class="example-steps"><li>Search the approved process documents available to that teammate.</li><li>Draft an answer using the relevant material.</li><li>Include the source so the person can verify the details.</li><li>Route missing or conflicting information to a process owner.</li></ol></div></section>
